course: dictionary
lesson: 7943
title: Reasonably: fair, sensible, not too much
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Most philosophers think we can be reasonably sure we're awake because waking life hangs together, but complete proof is hard.» Here reasonably means fairly or quite sure, but not totally sure. The sentence says proof is hard, so we cannot be completely certain.
explain: Reasonably comes from reason, so it describes something fair, sensible or calm. Adults ask children to «act reasonably», which means think fairly about what is right. Shops use it too: «reasonably priced» means the price is fair, not too expensive.
explain: Always check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «fairly» or «sensibly» in place of reasonably. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Reasonably means fairly or quite, but not very: «The test was reasonably easy» means it was not hard, but it was not very easy either.
fact: Reasonably also means in a sensible, fair or calm way: «Please behave reasonably.»
fact: «Reasonably priced» means not too expensive, so it is a fair price for what you get.
fact: In maths, reasonably close means near enough to be a sensible answer. Always check that your answer is reasonable.
fact: Useful phrases: «reasonably good», «reasonably sure», «reasonably priced» and «act reasonably».
exercise: «The test was reasonably easy.» What does reasonably mean here? | Not at all | Extremely | Very quickly | *Fairly or quite, but not very
exercise: «Please behave reasonably.» What does reasonably mean here? | *In a sensible, fair and calm way | Without any thought at all | In a loud and silly way | Slowly and quietly
exercise: A shop sign says «reasonably priced». What does it tell you about the prices? | They are the most expensive in town | They change every hour | *They are fair, not too expensive | They are free
test: «We can be reasonably sure we're awake, but complete proof is hard.» In this sentence, reasonably means | Not sure at all | *Not fully, but fairly sure | Happily and with a smile | Completely, with no doubt at all
test: Sam estimates 19 × 5 as 100. Is his answer reasonably close to the real answer, 95? | Only if the answer is exactly 95 | It has no meaning in maths at all | *Yes, it is near enough to be a sensible answer | No, it is nowhere near the right size
exam: «It can reasonably be expected that the shop will be open on Sunday.» What does reasonably add to this sentence? | *It shows a fair, sensible expectation, not a certainty | It shows the shop is definitely closed | It turns the sentence into a question | It means the shop is cheap
